collab/1790499500000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice4-r2.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790499500000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice4-r2.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790500926405-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice4-gate.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1790500926405-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice4-gate.gpt-6-sol.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790500926405-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice4-gate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790500930000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice4-fixes.glm-flash.report.md\n?? collab/1790500930000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice4-fixes.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790502000000-architect-repl-auto-index-design.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1790502148751-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice6.claude.findings.md\n?? collab/1790502148751-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice6.claude.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790502148751-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice6.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790502300000-architect-link-policy-gate.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1790502837067-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice4-confirm.gpt-6-sol.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790502837067-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice4-confirm.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790502859625-architect-link-policy-gate.gpt-6-sol.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790502859625-architect-link-policy-gate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790502989424-architect-ucf-pins-gate.gpt-6-sol.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790502989424-architect-ucf-pins-gate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790505140337-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice6-gate.gpt-6-sol.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790505140337-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice6-gate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log\n?? public/chp/blog/muse-ai-agent-on-datom-world.blog\n?? src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj\n?? src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj\n?? src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc\n?? src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd\n?? src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs\n?? test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc\n?? test/dao/stream/ws_project_cross_jvm_test.clj\n?? test/dao/stream/ws_project_cross_node_test.cljs\n?? test/dao/stream/ws_project_peer.cljc\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp\n     2\t  \"The UDP channel of dao.stream.remote.md (3.2): the WebSocket deposit\n     3\t   model over a socket that retains nothing.\n     4\t\n     5\t   `make-port` composes the local socket's protocol state, host-free: a\n     6\t   host adapter feeds every received datagram to `receive!` with its\n     7\t   raw bytes and the source address the socket saw. One CBOR value,\n     8\t   canonical profile, rides one datagram, at most `max-datagram` bytes,\n     9\t   fragment envelope included -- the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already\n    10\t   uses to clear common path MTUs. An encoded message over that budget\n    11\t   is split into fragment envelopes, channel-internal: neither the\n    12\t   mirror step nor the link (dao.stream.remote) ever sees one, only the\n    13\t   complete decoded message `receive!` reassembles and deposits.\n    14\t\n    15\t   Reassembly key, per 3.2: [attachment identity, source address,\n    16\t   direction, id]. For UDP the attachment identity a socket serves IS\n    17\t   the remote host:port it saw, so this port's own reassembly key\n    18\t   collapses the first two components into one address string; a\n    19\t   fragment's own carried id is unique only per asking side, so the\n    20\t   direction distinguishes a fragment split from an outgoing request\n    21\t   from one split from its answer, both landing at the same address\n    22\t   with the same id, when this side is both asker and mirror on one\n    23\t   channel. Bounded by two composition data, no clock:\n    24\t   `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB) refuses an\n    25\t   oversize send and drops an oversize reassembly claim; and\n    26\t   `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n    27\t   message.\n    28\t\n    29\t   The deposit adapter shape: every reassembled datagram value is\n    30\t   deposited as one event, `{:dao.stream.udp/attachment id\n    31\t   :dao.stream.udp/source {:dao.stream.udp/host h\n    32\t   :dao.stream.udp/port p} :dao.stream.udp/value v}`, onto the shared\n    33\t   traffic medium the composition wires -- one socket may serve several\n    34\t   attachments, one per remote address, so this medium is shared the\n    35\t   way a WebSocket connection's own traffic medium is not. `projection`\n    36\t   and `step!` are this channel's `ws-project`: driven per attachment\n    37\t   at the composition's cadence, they keep the events naming their own\n    38\t   attachment and append each value onto the channel ring that\n    39\t   attachment's reflections read.\n    40\t\n    41\t   `make-attacher` binds or reuses `:port` and answers a writer handle\n    42\t   whose `append!` sends toward the descriptor's address, fragmenting\n    43\t   and refusing oversize per the above; `send-to!` is the same write\n    44\t   path to an explicit destination from an already-bound port, for hole\n    45\t   punching (section 4), outside any attach!'d descriptor. Replies\n    46\t   always go to a datagram's own source address, never a payload's\n    47\t   claim, because `receive!` is the only place a source address is\n    48\t   read, and every deposited event carries the address the socket\n    49\t   itself saw it arrive from.\"\n    50\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    51\t            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor])\n    52\t  #?(:cljd (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])))\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\t(def transport-type :dao.stream/udp)\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(def max-datagram\n    59\t  \"The whole encoded datagram's budget, fragment envelope included: the\n    60\t   1200-byte figure dao.jing.dht.node already uses to clear common path\n    61\t   MTUs (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md 3.2).\"\n    62\t  1200)\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\t(def default-max-message-bytes\n    66\t  \"64 KiB, the default reassembly maximum (3.2).\"\n    67\t  (* 64 1024))\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t(def default-max-partial-messages\n    71\t  \"No default is named in 3.2; this is this implementation's own bound\n    72\t   on outstanding partial messages, chosen only to keep reassembly\n    73\t   memory finite without a clock.\"\n    74\t  64)\n    75\t\n    76\t\n    77\t(defn descriptor?\n    78\t  \"{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity id\n    79\t     :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p} (3.2).\"\n    80\t  [x]\n    81\t  (and (map? x)\n    82\t       (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type x))\n    83\t       (string? (:dao.stream/identity x))\n    84\t       (string? (:dao.stream.udp/host x))\n    85\t       (integer? (:dao.stream.udp/port x))\n    86\t       (pos? (:dao.stream.udp/port x))))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(defn address-key\n    90\t  \"The attachment identity a socket serves for one remote address: the\n    91\t   host:port it saw, as a string, so it can key a map.\"\n    92\t  [host port]\n    93\t  (str host \":\" port))\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t;; =============================================================================\n    97\t;; Portable host byte arrays\n    98\t;; =============================================================================\n    99\t\n   100\t(defn- blen\n   101\t  [bs]\n   102\t  #?(:cljd (.-length ^Uint8List bs)\n   103\t     :default (alength bs)))\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn- balloc\n   107\t  [n]\n   108\t  #?(:cljd (Uint8List. n)\n   109\t     :clj (byte-array n)\n   110\t     :cljs (js/Uint8Array. n)))\n   111\t\n   112\t\n   113\t(defn- bslice\n   114\t  [bs start end]\n   115\t  #?(:cljd (Uint8List.fromList (.sublist ^Uint8List bs start end))\n   116\t     :clj (java.util.Arrays/copyOfRange ^bytes bs (int start) (int end))\n   117\t     :cljs (.slice bs start end)))\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\t(defn- bconcat\n   121\t  [chunks]\n   122\t  (let [total (reduce + (map blen chunks))\n   123\t        out (balloc total)]\n   124\t    (reduce (fn [off c]\n   125\t              (let [n (blen c)]\n   126\t                #?(:cljd (.setRange ^Uint8List out off (+ off n) c)\n   127\t                   :clj (System/arraycopy c 0 out off n)\n   128\t                   :cljs (.set out c off))\n   129\t                (+ off n)))\n   130\t            0 chunks)\n   131\t    out))\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\t(defn- ceil-div\n   135\t  [a b]\n   136\t  (quot (+ a (dec b)) b))\n   137\t\n   138\t\n   139\t;; =============================================================================\n   140\t;; Fragmentation (write side)\n   141\t;; =============================================================================\n   142\t\n   143\t(defn- direction-of\n   144\t  \"A request carries :dao.stream.remote/op; an answer never does.\"\n   145\t  [v]\n   146\t  (if (contains? v :dao.stream.remote/op)\n   147\t    :dao.stream.udp/request\n   148\t    :dao.stream.udp/answer))\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\t(defn- fragment-envelope\n   152\t  [id part parts direction chunk]\n   153\t  {:dao.stream.remote/id id\n   154\t   :dao.stream.udp/part part\n   155\t   :dao.stream.udp/parts parts\n   156\t   :dao.stream.udp/direction direction\n   157\t   :dao.stream.udp/bytes chunk})\n   158\t\n   159\t\n   160\t(defn- fits?\n   161\t  [id part parts direction chunk-len]\n   162\t  (<= (blen (cbor/encode\n   163\t              (fragment-envelope id part parts direction\n   164\t                                 (balloc chunk-len))))\n   165\t      max-datagram))\n   166\t\n   167\t\n   168\t(defn- chunk-size\n   169\t  \"The largest chunk length whose fragment envelope, at the highest\n   170\t   part index (the widest of the small integers), still fits the\n   171\t   datagram budget; backs off from an optimistic start.\"\n   172\t  [id parts direction]\n   173\t  (loop [size (- max-datagram 16)]\n   174\t    (when (pos? size)\n   175\t      (if (fits? id (dec parts) parts direction size)\n   176\t        size\n   177\t        (recur (dec size))))))\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\t(defn- fragments\n   181\t  \"Split encoded `payload` for message `id`, direction `direction`,\n   182\t   into fragment envelopes that each fit the datagram budget.\"\n   183\t  [payload id direction]\n   184\t  (let [total (blen payload)\n   185\t        parts (max 1 (ceil-div total (- max-datagram 16)))\n   186\t        size (or (chunk-size id parts direction)\n   187\t                 (throw (ex-info\n   188\t                          \"dao.stream.udp: no chunk size fits the budget\"\n   189\t                          {:id id :parts parts})))\n   190\t        parts (max parts (ceil-div total size))\n   191\t        size (or (chunk-size id parts direction) size)]\n   192\t    (for [i (range parts)\n   193\t          :let [start (* i size)\n   194\t                end (min total (+ start size))]]\n   195\t      (fragment-envelope id i parts direction (bslice payload start end)))))\n   196\t\n   197\t\n   198\t;; =============================================================================\n   199\t;; The port: shared local-socket protocol state\n   200\t;; =============================================================================\n   201\t\n   202\t(defn make-port\n   203\t  \"Compose one local socket's protocol state. `:send!` is the host\n   204\t   seam, `(send! host port bytes) -> nil`, called once per outbound\n   205\t   datagram; it must not wait or throw for an ordinary transport\n   206\t   failure. `:traffic` is the shared deposit target the composition\n   207\t   wires -- a writer every reassembled datagram's event is appended to\n   208\t   (`dao.stream/writer?`). `:max-message-bytes` and\n   209\t   `:max-partial-messages` are the two reassembly bounds (defaults\n   210\t   above).\"\n   211\t  [{:keys [send! traffic max-message-bytes max-partial-messages]}]\n   212\t  (when-not (and (fn? send!) (stream/writer? traffic))\n   213\t    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp port composition\"\n   214\t                    {:send! send! :traffic traffic})))\n   215\t  (atom {:send! send!\n   216\t         :traffic traffic\n   217\t         :max-message-bytes (or max-message-bytes\n   218\t                                default-max-message-bytes)\n   219\t         :max-partial-messages (or max-partial-messages\n   220\t                                   default-max-partial-messages)\n   221\t         :partial {}\n   222\t         :partial-order []}))\n   223\t\n   224\t\n   225\t(defn- send-datagram!\n   226\t  [port host dest-port bytes]\n   227\t  ((:send! @port) host dest-port bytes))\n   228\t\n   229\t\n   230\t(defn send-value!\n   231\t  \"Encode and send `v` toward `host`:`dest-port` from `port`'s socket:\n   232\t   a single datagram when it fits the budget, fragments when it does\n   233\t   not, transport-error naming oversize when the encoded message\n   234\t   exceeds :dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes -- never sent, torn or\n   235\t   partially sent.\"\n   236\t  [port host dest-port v]\n   237\t  (let [payload (cbor/encode v)\n   238\t        n (blen payload)]\n   239\t    (cond\n   240\t      (> n (:max-message-bytes @port))\n   241\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   242\t       :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/oversize}\n   243\t\n   244\t      (<= n max-datagram)\n   245\t      (do (send-datagram! port host dest-port payload)\n   246\t          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})\n   247\t\n   248\t      :else\n   249\t      (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id v)\n   250\t            direction (direction-of v)]\n   251\t        (doseq [frag (fragments payload id direction)]\n   252\t          (send-datagram! port host dest-port (cbor/encode frag)))\n   253\t        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))))\n   254\t\n   255\t\n   256\t(defn send-to!\n   257\t  \"The explicit-destination write path from an already-bound port,\n   258\t   outside any attach!'d descriptor -- hole punching depends on it\n   259\t   (3.2, section 4).\"\n   260\t  [port host dest-port v]\n   261\t  (send-value! port host dest-port v))\n   262\t\n   263\t\n   264\t;; =============================================================================\n   265\t;; Reassembly (read side)\n   266\t;; =============================================================================\n   267\t\n   268\t(defn- fragment-envelope?\n   269\t  [v]\n   270\t  (and (map? v) (contains? v :dao.stream.udp/part)))\n   271\t\n   272\t\n   273\t(defn- evict-oldest!\n   274\t  [port]\n   275\t  (let [order (:partial-order @port)]\n   276\t    (when (seq order)\n   277\t      (let [k (first order)]\n   278\t        (swap! port (fn [s]\n   279\t                      (-> s\n   280\t                          (update :partial dissoc k)\n   281\t                          (update :partial-order subvec 1))))))))\n   282\t\n   283\t\n   284\t(defn- absorb-fragment!\n   285\t  \"Fold one fragment envelope into the port's reassembly state, keyed\n   286\t   by [attachment direction id]; returns the complete decoded value\n   287\t   once every part has arrived, else nil. An oversize claim (declared\n   288\t   parts times the datagram budget beyond max-message-bytes) is\n   289\t   dropped before a buffer for it exists, so a hostile or mistaken\n   290\t   `:dao.stream.udp/parts` cannot grow reassembly past the bound.\"\n   291\t  [port attachment frag]\n   292\t  (let [{:dao.stream.remote/keys [id]\n   293\t         :dao.stream.udp/keys [part parts direction bytes]} frag\n   294\t        k [attachment direction id]]\n   295\t    (when (or (contains? (:partial @port) k)\n   296\t              (<= (* parts max-datagram) (:max-message-bytes @port)))\n   297\t      (when-not (contains? (:partial @port) k)\n   298\t        (swap! port (fn [s]\n   299\t                      (-> s\n   300\t                          (assoc-in [:partial k]\n   301\t                                    {:parts {} :parts-count parts})\n   302\t                          (update :partial-order conj k))))\n   303\t        (when (> (count (:partial @port))\n   304\t                 (:max-partial-messages @port))\n   305\t          (evict-oldest! port)))\n   306\t      (when (contains? (:partial @port) k)\n   307\t        (swap! port assoc-in [:partial k :parts part] bytes)\n   308\t        (let [entry (get (:partial @port) k)]\n   309\t          (when (= (count (:parts entry)) (:parts-count entry))\n   310\t            (swap! port (fn [s]\n   311\t                          (-> s\n   312\t                              (update :partial dissoc k)\n   313\t                              (update :partial-order\n   314\t                                      (fn [order] (vec (remove #{k} order)))))))\n   315\t            (bconcat (mapv (:parts entry) (range (:parts-count entry))))))))))\n   316\t\n   317\t\n   318\t(defn- deposit!\n   319\t  [port attachment host src-port v]\n   320\t  (stream/append! (:traffic @port)\n   321\t                  {:dao.stream.udp/attachment attachment\n   322\t                   :dao.stream.udp/source {:dao.stream.udp/host host\n   323\t                                           :dao.stream.udp/port src-port}\n   324\t                   :dao.stream.udp/value v}))\n   325\t\n   326\t\n   327\t(defn receive!\n   328\t  \"Adapter entry: one raw datagram arrived from `host`:`src-port` on\n   329\t   `port`'s socket, as `bytes`. Malformed CBOR is dropped as a\n   330\t   diagnostic below the mirror and the link, per dao.stream.remote.md\n   331\t   2.1. A plain value deposits at once; a fragment envelope folds into\n   332\t   reassembly and deposits only once complete -- loss of any part is\n   333\t   loss of the whole message, recovered by the link's resend rule, not\n   334\t   by this port. Replies to what this call learns go to `host`:\n   335\t   `src-port`, this datagram's own source, never an address a payload\n   336\t   might claim.\"\n   337\t  [port host src-port bytes]\n   338\t  (when-some [v (try (cbor/decode bytes) (catch #?(:cljd Object\n   339\t                                                   :clj Throwable\n   340\t                                                   :cljs :default) _\n   341\t                                           nil))]\n   342\t    (let [attachment (address-key host src-port)]\n   343\t      (if (fragment-envelope? v)\n   344\t        (when-some [payload (absorb-fragment! port attachment v)]\n   345\t          (when-some [whole (try (cbor/decode payload)\n   346\t                                 (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable\n   347\t                                           :cljs :default) _\n   348\t                                   nil))]\n   349\t            (deposit! port attachment host src-port whole)))\n   350\t        (deposit! port attachment host src-port v)))))\n   351\t\n   352\t\n   353\t;; =============================================================================\n   354\t;; The projection: one channel ring per attachment (this channel's\n   355\t;; ws-project)\n   356\t;; =============================================================================\n   357\t\n   358\t(defn projection\n   359\t  \"Compose one projection over the attachment identity `:attachment`\n   360\t   (an `address-key`), the shared traffic reader `:traffic` this port\n   361\t   deposits onto, the already-minted reading cursor `:cursor`, and the\n   362\t   channel ring `:ring` this attachment's reflections and mirror steps\n   363\t   read. Owns the cursor from here: each `step!` advances it.\"\n   364\t  [{:keys [attachment traffic cursor ring]}]\n   365\t  (when-not (and (string? attachment)\n   366\t                 (stream/reader? traffic)\n   367\t                 (some? cursor)\n   368\t                 (stream/reader? ring)\n   369\t                 (stream/writer? ring))\n   370\t    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp projection composition\"\n   371\t                    {:attachment attachment :traffic traffic\n   372\t                     :cursor cursor :ring ring})))\n   373\t  (atom {:attachment attachment :traffic traffic :cursor cursor\n   374\t         :ring ring}))\n   375\t\n   376\t\n   377\t(defn step!\n   378\t  \"Drive the projection once, at the composition's cadence: read the\n   379\t   traffic medium to blocked, keeping this attachment's own events and\n   380\t   dropping every other attachment's, appending each kept value onto\n   381\t   the channel ring. A gap adopts the medium's own recovery cursor.\n   382\t   Returns the projection.\"\n   383\t  [project]\n   384\t  (loop []\n   385\t    (let [s @project\n   386\t          r (stream/next (:traffic s) (:cursor s))]\n   387\t      (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   388\t        :dao.stream/ok\n   389\t        (let [event (:dao.stream/value r)]\n   390\t          (swap! project assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   391\t          (when (= (:attachment s) (:dao.stream.udp/attachment event))\n   392\t            (stream/append! (:ring s) (:dao.stream.udp/value event)))\n   393\t          (recur))\n   394\t\n   395\t        :dao.stream/gap\n   396\t        (do (swap! project assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   397\t            (recur))\n   398\t\n   399\t        project))))\n   400\t\n   401\t\n   402\t(defn reading-cursor\n   403\t  [project]\n   404\t  (:cursor @project))\n   405\t\n   406\t\n   407\t;; =============================================================================\n   408\t;; The writer handle and attacher\n   409\t;; =============================================================================\n   410\t\n   411\t(deftype UdpHandle\n   412\t  [port host dest-port descriptor]\n   413\t\n   414\t  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n   415\t\n   416\t  (descriptor\n   417\t    [_]\n   418\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   419\t     :dao.stream/descriptor descriptor\n   420\t     :dao.stream/identity (:dao.stream/identity descriptor)})\n   421\t\n   422\t\n   423\t  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n   424\t\n   425\t  (append!\n   426\t    [_ v]\n   427\t    (send-value! port host dest-port v)))\n   428\t\n   429\t\n   430\t(defn make-attacher\n   431\t  \"Construct the host-composed unary `attach!` entry point: `:port` is\n   432\t   an already-composed `make-port` value. `attach!` binds or reuses it\n   433\t   -- one port serves every descriptor a composition attaches through\n   434\t   it -- and answers at once with a writer handle whose `append!` sends\n   435\t   toward the descriptor's address.\"\n   436\t  [{:keys [port] :as config}]\n   437\t  (when-not (some? port)\n   438\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.stream.udp attacher needs :port\" {:config config})))\n   439\t  (fn attach!\n   440\t    [descriptor]\n   441\t    (if-not (descriptor? descriptor)\n   442\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor}\n   443\t      (let [host (:dao.stream.udp/host descriptor)\n   444\t            dest-port (:dao.stream.udp/port descriptor)]\n   445\t        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   446\t         :dao.stream/handle (UdpHandle. port host dest-port descriptor)\n   447\t         :dao.stream/attachment (address-key host dest-port)}))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '352,390p' docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; cat collab/1790502148751-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice6.claude.findings.md; for f in src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc; do nl -ba \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '352,390p' docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; cat collab/1790502148751-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice6.claude.findings.md; for f in src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc; do nl -ba \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"### 3.2 UDP\n\n`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\nretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\ndatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\nvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n\n- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n  path is datagrams to that address.\n- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n  split into `k` datagrams, each\n  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n  source-address, direction, id]`, because ids are unique only per channel\n  reader and one socket may serve several attachments, and delivers only a\n  complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole message,\n  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n  composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB)\n  and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n  in a payload.\n- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n  punching depends on it (section 4).\n- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n  republishes.\n\nHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n\n### 3.3 Pair\n\nSlice 6 (UDP channel) implemented as new files only:\n\n- `src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc` — the channel: `descriptor?`, `address-key`, `make-port`/`send-value!`/`send-to!` (write side: single-datagram fast path, fragmentation over the 1200-byte budget, `:dao.stream/transport-error` + `:dao.stream.remote/oversize` refusal beyond `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes`, default 64 KiB), `receive!` (reassembly + the deposit-event shape), `projection`/`step!` (this channel's `ws-project`, one channel ring per attachment), `make-attacher`/`UdpHandle`.\n- `src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj`, `src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd` — thin socket adapters (DatagramSocket / dgram / RawDatagramSocket); no protocol logic.\n- `test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc` — 10 portable host-free tests: fragmentation/reassembly round trip, the 48 KiB toy inside the 64 KiB default, loss-of-any-part + resend recovery, oversize refusal (unsent), reassembly eviction (oldest first), source-address carriage, keying by [attachment, direction, id] with no cross-delivery, and per-attachment projection routing.\n\nMinimal-reading notes (genuinely ambiguous points in 3.2):\n- The design's reassembly key lists `[attachment identity, source address, direction, id]`, but the literal fragment-envelope shape it shows carries only 4 keys. For UDP, attachment identity *is* the remote host:port (stated later in 3.2), so identity and source collapse to one string; I added `:dao.stream.udp/direction` (`:request`/`:answer`, derived from `:dao.stream.remote/op`'s presence) to the envelope, since without it two fragmenting messages sharing an id and address (this peer as both asker and mirror) would corrupt each other's reassembly.\n- `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages` has no stated default (only max-message-bytes does); I chose 64 as this implementation's own bound and documented it as such.\n- The design describes one deposit medium plus a `ws-project`-style per-attachment forwarding step; I implemented both (`receive!` deposits, `projection`/`step!` forwards), matching item 1's \"deposit adapter shape\" literally.\n\nVerify, sequential and solo:\n- JVM: `clj -M:test` → 2276 tests / 183262 assertions / 0 failures, 0 errors (baseline 2254/183121/0 plus other in-flight sessions' own added tests plus mine; no failures anywhere, `dao.stream.udp-test` included and green).\n- Node: `bb test:cljs` → 2185/49891/0, `dao.stream.udp-test` ran and passed.\n- Dart: `bb test:cljd` (after `rm -rf test/cljd-out`) → 2145 tests, \"All tests passed!\", `dao.stream.udp-test`'s 10 tests all green; fixed two DYNAMIC WARNINGs in `dart.cljd` (untyped `.then` callback params) by type-hinting `socket`/`datagram`.\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint` clean (0 errors, 0 warnings) on every new file, including the `.cljd` one.\n- Pure ASCII, ≤80 columns verified on every new file.\n- `cljstyle` could not be run in this session (the tool required an approval this non-interactive session couldn't grant); formatting was instead hand-matched to `dao.stream.remote.cljc`/`dao.stream.ws-project.cljc`'s existing indentation conventions.\n- No commit/stage/checkout/reset/stash; only the five listed new files plus the fix to `dart.cljd` were touched; a scratch debug script used to diagnose one test bug was deleted before finishing.\n\nStatus: COMPLETE\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp.jvm\n     2\t  \"The JVM host edge for dao.stream.udp: one DatagramSocket, bound once,\n     3\t   with a daemon receiver thread that feeds every datagram's source\n     4\t   address and raw bytes to a composed `receive-fn`\n     5\t   (dao.stream.udp/receive!, partially applied over the port), and the\n     6\t   :send! seam dao.stream.udp/make-port composes over. All protocol\n     7\t   logic -- fragmentation, reassembly, the deposit shape -- stays in\n     8\t   dao.stream.udp; this adapter only owns the socket and its thread.\"\n     9\t  (:import (java.net DatagramPacket DatagramSocket InetAddress)))\n    10\t\n    11\t\n    12\t(defn bind!\n    13\t  \"Bind a DatagramSocket at `port` (0 for an ephemeral port) and start\n    14\t   its daemon receiver thread. Returns {:socket s :local-port p :send!\n    15\t   f :close! f}; `:send!` is `(send! host dest-port bytes) -> nil`,\n    16\t   the port composition's host seam.\"\n    17\t  ([receive-fn] (bind! receive-fn 0))\n    18\t  ([receive-fn port]\n    19\t   (let [socket (DatagramSocket. (int port))\n    20\t         running (atom true)\n    21\t         thread\n    22\t         (Thread.\n    23\t           (fn []\n    24\t             (let [buf (byte-array 65536)\n    25\t                   packet (DatagramPacket. buf (alength buf))]\n    26\t               (while @running\n    27\t                 (when (try (.receive socket packet)\n    28\t                            true\n    29\t                            (catch Exception _\n    30\t                              (when @running (Thread/sleep 10))\n    31\t                              false))\n    32\t                   (let [n (.getLength packet)\n    33\t                         bytes (java.util.Arrays/copyOfRange\n    34\t                                 (.getData packet) 0 n)\n    35\t                         host (.getHostAddress (.getAddress packet))\n    36\t                         src-port (.getPort packet)]\n    37\t                     (try (receive-fn host src-port bytes)\n    38\t                          (catch Exception _ nil))))))))]\n    39\t     (.setDaemon thread true)\n    40\t     (.start thread)\n    41\t     {:socket socket\n    42\t      :local-port (.getLocalPort socket)\n    43\t      :send! (fn [host dest-port bytes]\n    44\t               (.send socket\n    45\t                      (DatagramPacket. bytes (alength bytes)\n    46\t                                       (InetAddress/getByName host)\n    47\t                                       (int dest-port)))\n    48\t               nil)\n    49\t      :close! (fn []\n    50\t                (reset! running false)\n    51\t                (.close socket)\n    52\t                nil)})))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp.node\n     2\t  \"The Node host edge for dao.stream.udp: one `dgram` socket, bound\n     3\t   once, whose \\\"message\\\" event feeds every datagram's source address\n     4\t   and raw bytes to a composed `receive-fn` (dao.stream.udp/receive!,\n     5\t   partially applied over the port), and the :send! seam\n     6\t   dao.stream.udp/make-port composes over. All protocol logic --\n     7\t   fragmentation, reassembly, the deposit shape -- stays in\n     8\t   dao.stream.udp; this adapter only owns the socket. A Node Buffer\n     9\t   already satisfies dao.stream.cbor's byte-payload? (it extends\n    10\t   Uint8Array), so a received message reaches `receive-fn` unconverted.\"\n    11\t  (:require [\"dgram\" :as dgram]))\n    12\t\n    13\t\n    14\t(defn bind!\n    15\t  \"Bind a UDP socket at `port` (0 for an ephemeral port). Returns\n    16\t   {:socket s :local-port p :send! f :close! f} once bound; `on-bound`\n    17\t   is called with that map, because Node's bind is asynchronous.\"\n    18\t  ([receive-fn on-bound] (bind! receive-fn 0 on-bound))\n    19\t  ([receive-fn port on-bound]\n    20\t   (let [socket (.createSocket dgram \"udp4\")]\n    21\t     (.on socket \"message\"\n    22\t          (fn [msg rinfo]\n    23\t            (receive-fn (.-address rinfo) (.-port rinfo) msg)))\n    24\t     (.on socket \"listening\"\n    25\t          (fn []\n    26\t            (let [address (.address socket)]\n    27\t              (on-bound\n    28\t                {:socket socket\n    29\t                 :local-port (.-port address)\n    30\t                 :send! (fn [host dest-port bytes]\n    31\t                          (.send socket bytes dest-port host)\n    32\t                          nil)\n    33\t                 :close! (fn [] (.close socket) nil)}))))\n    34\t     (.bind socket port))))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp.dart\n     2\t  \"The dart:io host edge for dao.stream.udp: one RawDatagramSocket,\n     3\t   bound once, whose event stream feeds every datagram's source\n     4\t   address and raw bytes to a composed `receive-fn`\n     5\t   (dao.stream.udp/receive!, partially applied over the port), and the\n     6\t   :send! seam dao.stream.udp/make-port composes over. All protocol\n     7\t   logic -- fragmentation, reassembly, the deposit shape -- stays in\n     8\t   dao.stream.udp; this adapter only owns the socket.\"\n     9\t  (:require [\"dart:async\" :as async]\n    10\t            [\"dart:io\" :as io]))\n    11\t\n    12\t\n    13\t(defn bind!\n    14\t  \"Bind a RawDatagramSocket at `port` (0 for an ephemeral port).\n    15\t   Returns a Future of {:socket s :local-port p :send! f :close! f}.\"\n    16\t  ([receive-fn] (bind! receive-fn 0))\n    17\t  ([receive-fn port]\n    18\t   (-> ^async/Future (io/RawDatagramSocket.bind \"0.0.0.0\" port)\n    19\t       (.then\n    20\t         (fn [socket]\n    21\t           (let [socket ^io/RawDatagramSocket socket]\n    22\t             (.listen socket\n    23\t                      (fn [event]\n    24\t                        (when (= event io/RawSocketEvent.read)\n    25\t                          (when-some [^io/Datagram datagram\n    26\t                                      (.receive socket)]\n    27\t                            (receive-fn\n    28\t                              (.-address ^io/InternetAddress\n    29\t                               (.-address datagram))\n    30\t                              (.-port datagram)\n    31\t                              (.-data datagram))))))\n    32\t             {:socket socket\n    33\t              :local-port (.-port socket)\n    34\t              :send! (fn [host dest-port bytes]\n    35\t                       (.send socket bytes (io/InternetAddress. host)\n    36\t                              dest-port)\n    37\t                       nil)\n    38\t              :close! (fn [] (.close socket) nil)}))))))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp-test\n     2\t  \"Portable, host-free protocol tests for dao.stream.udp\n     3\t   (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md 3.2): fragmentation and\n     4\t   reassembly round trip; loss of any part loses the message,\n     5\t   recovered by resend; oversize refuses transport-error naming\n     6\t   oversize, unsent; reassembly eviction, oldest partial message\n     7\t   first; the 48 KiB toy inside the default 64 KiB maximum; the\n     8\t   deposited event carries the datagram's own source address; and\n     9\t   keying by [attachment, direction, id] -- two attachments, equal\n    10\t   ids, no cross-delivery -- including the projection that routes the\n    11\t   shared traffic medium onto each attachment's own channel ring.\"\n    12\t  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n    13\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\n    14\t            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor]\n    15\t            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\n    16\t            [dao.stream.udp :as udp]))\n    17\t\n    18\t\n    19\t(defn- ring\n    20\t  [capacity]\n    21\t  (:dao.stream/handle\n    22\t    (ringbuffer/create! {:dao.stream/type ringbuffer/transport-type\n    23\t                         ringbuffer/capacity-key capacity})))\n    24\t\n    25\t\n    26\t(defn- spy\n    27\t  \"A :send! seam recording every send as [host port bytes], touching\n    28\t   no real socket.\"\n    29\t  []\n    30\t  (let [log (atom [])]\n    31\t    {:log log\n    32\t     :send! (fn [host port bytes] (swap! log conj [host port bytes]))}))\n    33\t\n    34\t\n    35\t(defn- values\n    36\t  \"Every retained value of `h`, oldest first.\"\n    37\t  [h]\n    38\t  (loop [c (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/cursor h stream/anchor-oldest))\n    39\t         acc []]\n    40\t    (let [r (stream/next h c)]\n    41\t      (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n    42\t        (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r) (conj acc (:dao.stream/value r)))\n    43\t        acc))))\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\t(defn- deposited\n    47\t  \"Every deposit event's own carried :dao.stream.udp/value, oldest\n    48\t   first -- what a projection would forward onto a channel ring.\"\n    49\t  [traffic]\n    50\t  (mapv :dao.stream.udp/value (values traffic)))\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t(defn- big-string\n    54\t  [n]\n    55\t  (apply str (repeat n \\a)))\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(defn- request\n    59\t  [identity id v]\n    60\t  {:dao.stream/identity identity\n    61\t   :dao.stream.remote/op :dao.stream/append!\n    62\t   :dao.stream.remote/args [v]\n    63\t   :dao.stream.remote/id id})\n    64\t\n    65\t\n    66\t;; =============================================================================\n    67\t;; descriptor? and address-key\n    68\t;; =============================================================================\n    69\t\n    70\t(deftest descriptor-gate\n    71\t  (is (udp/descriptor? {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp\n    72\t                        :dao.stream/identity \"peer-a\"\n    73\t                        :dao.stream.udp/host \"127.0.0.1\"\n    74\t                        :dao.stream.udp/port 9000}))\n    75\t  (is (not (udp/descriptor? {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp\n    76\t                             :dao.stream/identity \"peer-a\"\n    77\t                             :dao.stream.udp/host \"127.0.0.1\"\n    78\t                             :dao.stream.udp/port 0})))\n    79\t  (is (not (udp/descriptor? {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws\n    80\t                             :dao.stream/identity \"peer-a\"\n    81\t                             :dao.stream.udp/host \"h\"\n    82\t                             :dao.stream.udp/port 1}))))\n    83\t\n    84\t\n    85\t(deftest address-key-format\n    86\t  (is (= \"127.0.0.1:9000\" (udp/address-key \"127.0.0.1\" 9000))))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t;; =============================================================================\n    90\t;; Fragmentation and reassembly round trip\n    91\t;; =============================================================================\n    92\t\n    93\t(deftest fragmentation-round-trip\n    94\t  (testing \"a value over the datagram budget arrives whole\"\n    95\t    (let [traffic (ring 64)\n    96\t          {:keys [send! log]} (spy)\n    97\t          port (udp/make-port {:send! send! :traffic traffic})\n    98\t          v (request \"s\" 1 (big-string 4000))\n    99\t          sent (do (udp/send-value! port \"peer\" 9000 v)\n   100\t                   @log)\n   101\t          receiver (udp/make-port\n   102\t                     {:send! (fn [& _] nil) :traffic traffic})]\n   103\t      (is (> (count sent) 1) \"the value did not fit one datagram\")\n   104\t      (doseq [[_ _ bytes] sent]\n   105\t        (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 bytes))\n   106\t      (is (= [v] (deposited traffic))))))\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   109\t(deftest fragmentation-round-trip-48kib\n   110\t  (testing \"the 48 KiB toy, inside the default 64 KiB maximum\"\n   111\t    (let [traffic (ring 64)\n   112\t          {:keys [send! log]} (spy)\n   113\t          port (udp/make-port {:send! send! :traffic traffic})\n   114\t          v (request \"s\" 2 (big-string (* 48 1024)))\n   115\t          sent (do (udp/send-value! port \"peer\" 9000 v)\n   116\t                   @log)\n   117\t          receiver (udp/make-port\n   118\t                     {:send! (fn [& _] nil) :traffic traffic})]\n   119\t      (doseq [[_ _ bytes] sent]\n   120\t        (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 bytes))\n   121\t      (is (= [v] (deposited traffic))))))\n   122\t\n   123\t\n   124\t;; =============================================================================\n   125\t;; Loss of any part loses the message, recovered by resend\n   126\t;; =============================================================================\n   127\t\n   128\t(deftest loss-of-any-part-loses-the-message\n   129\t  (testing \"dropping one datagram loses the whole message\"\n   130\t    (let [traffic (ring 64)\n   131\t          {:keys [send! log]} (spy)\n   132\t          port (udp/make-port {:send! send! :traffic traffic})\n   133\t          v (request \"s\" 3 (big-string 4000))\n   134\t          sent (do (udp/send-value! port \"peer\" 9000 v)\n   135\t                   @log)\n   136\t          receiver (udp/make-port\n   137\t                     {:send! (fn [& _] nil) :traffic traffic})\n   138\t          dropped (rest sent)]\n   139\t      (is (> (count sent) 1))\n   140\t      (doseq [[_ _ bytes] dropped]\n   141\t        (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 bytes))\n   142\t      (is (= [] (deposited traffic))\n   143\t          \"the message never completes without every part\")\n   144\t      (testing \"the link's resend rule recovers it: re-send every part\"\n   145\t        (doseq [[_ _ bytes] sent]\n   146\t          (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 bytes))\n   147\t        (is (= [v] (deposited traffic)))))))\n   148\t\n   149\t\n   150\t;; =============================================================================\n   151\t;; Oversize refuses, unsent\n   152\t;; =============================================================================\n   153\t\n   154\t(deftest oversize-refuses-unsent\n   155\t  (testing \"a message beyond max-message-bytes is never sent\"\n   156\t    (let [traffic (ring 64)\n   157\t          {:keys [send! log]} (spy)\n   158\t          port (udp/make-port {:send! send! :traffic traffic\n   159\t                               :max-message-bytes 1000})\n   160\t          v (request \"s\" 4 (big-string 4000))\n   161\t          result (udp/send-value! port \"peer\" 9000 v)]\n   162\t      (is (= {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   163\t              :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/oversize}\n   164\t             result))\n   165\t      (is (= [] @log) \"nothing was sent, torn or partially sent\"))))\n   166\t\n   167\t\n   168\t;; =============================================================================\n   169\t;; Reassembly eviction: max-partial-messages, oldest evicted\n   170\t;; =============================================================================\n   171\t\n   172\t(deftest reassembly-eviction-evicts-oldest\n   173\t  (testing \"the oldest partial message is evicted over the bound\"\n   174\t    (let [traffic (ring 64)\n   175\t          {:keys [send! log]} (spy)\n   176\t          port (udp/make-port {:send! send! :traffic traffic\n   177\t                               :max-partial-messages 2})\n   178\t          v1 (request \"s\" 10 (big-string 4000))\n   179\t          v2 (request \"s\" 11 (big-string 4000))\n   180\t          v3 (request \"s\" 12 (big-string 4000))\n   181\t          sent! (fn [v]\n   182\t                  (reset! log [])\n   183\t                  (udp/send-value! port \"peer\" 9000 v)\n   184\t                  @log)\n   185\t          s1 (sent! v1)\n   186\t          s2 (sent! v2)\n   187\t          s3 (sent! v3)\n   188\t          receiver (udp/make-port\n   189\t                     {:send! (fn [& _] nil) :traffic traffic\n   190\t                      :max-partial-messages 2})]\n   191\t      (is (every? #(> (count %) 1) [s1 s2 s3]))\n   192\t      ;; withhold the last part of each, so none completes yet, and the\n   193\t      ;; oldest (v1's) partial is evicted once the third begins\n   194\t      (doseq [sent [s1 s2 s3]\n   195\t              [_ _ bytes] (butlast sent)]\n   196\t        (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 bytes))\n   197\t      ;; complete v1: evicted, so it never arrives\n   198\t      (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 (last (last s1)))\n   199\t      (is (= [] (deposited traffic)) \"v1's partial was evicted\")\n   200\t      ;; complete v3: kept, so it arrives\n   201\t      (udp/receive! receiver \"peer\" 9000 (last (last s3)))\n   202\t      (is (= [v3] (deposited traffic))))))\n   203\t\n   204\t\n   205\t;; =============================================================================\n   206\t;; The deposited event carries the datagram's own source address\n   207\t;; =============================================================================\n   208\t\n   209\t(deftest deposit-carries-source-address\n   210\t  (testing \"the event names the source the socket saw, for replies\"\n   211\t    (let [traffic (ring 64)\n   212\t          port (udp/make-port\n   213\t                 {:send! (fn [& _] nil) :traffic traffic})\n   214\t          v (request \"s\" 21 \"hello\")]\n   215\t      (udp/receive! port \"203.0.113.7\" 4242 (cbor/encode v))\n   216\t      (let [r (stream/next traffic\n   217\t                           (:dao.stream/cursor\n   218\t                             (stream/cursor traffic\n   219\t                                            stream/anchor-oldest)))\n   220\t            event (:dao.stream/value r)]\n   221\t        (is (= (udp/address-key \"203.0.113.7\" 4242)\n   222\t               (:dao.stream.udp/attachment event)))\n   223\t        (is (= {:dao.stream.udp/host \"203.0.113.7\"\n   224\t                :dao.stream.udp/port 4242}\n   225\t               (:dao.stream.udp/source event)))\n   226\t        (is (= v (:dao.stream.udp/value event)))))))\n   227\t\n   228\t\n   229\t;; =============================================================================\n   230\t;; Keying by [attachment, direction, id]: two attachments, equal ids,\n   231\t;; no cross-delivery\n   232\t;; =============================================================================\n   233\t\n   234\t(deftest keying-prevents-cross-delivery\n   235\t  (testing \"two attachments fragmenting the same id, interleaved\"\n   236\t    (let [traffic (ring 64)\n   237\t          {:keys [send! log]} (spy)\n   238\t          port-a (udp/make-port {:send! send! :traffic traffic})\n   239\t          port-b (udp/make-port {:send! send! :traffic traffic})\n   240\t          va (request \"s\" 99 (big-string 4000))\n   241\t          vb (request \"s\" 99 (big-string 3000))\n   242\t          sent-a (do (reset! log [])\n   243\t                     (udp/send-value! port-a \"peer-a\" 9001 va)\n   244\t                     @log)\n   245\t          sent-b (do (reset! log [])\n   246\t                     (udp/send-value! port-b \"peer-b\" 9002 vb)\n   247\t                     @log)\n   248\t          receiver (udp/make-port\n   249\t                     {:send! (fn [& _] nil) :traffic traffic})]\n   250\t      (is (> (count sent-a) 1))\n   251\t      (is (> (count sent-b) 1))\n   252\t      ;; interleave: alternate a and b datagrams\n   253\t      (loop [as sent-a bs sent-b]\n   254\t        (when (seq as)\n   255\t          (udp/receive! receiver \"peer-a\" 9001 (last (first as))))\n   256\t        (when (seq bs)\n   257\t          (udp/receive! receiver \"peer-b\" 9002 (last (first bs))))\n   258\t        (when (or (seq as) (seq bs))\n   259\t          (recur (rest as) (rest bs))))\n   260\t      (is (= #{va vb} (set (deposited traffic)))))))\n   261\t\n   262\t\n   263\t;; =============================================================================\n   264\t;; The projection: one channel ring per attachment\n   265\t;; =============================================================================\n   266\t\n   267\t(deftest projection-routes-by-attachment\n   268\t  (testing \"step! forwards only the projected attachment's own events\"\n   269\t    (let [traffic (ring 64)\n   270\t          port (udp/make-port\n   271\t                 {:send! (fn [& _] nil) :traffic traffic})\n   272\t          channel-a (ring 64)\n   273\t          channel-b (ring 64)\n   274\t          cursor (:dao.stream/cursor\n   275\t                   (stream/cursor traffic stream/anchor-oldest))\n   276\t          att-a (udp/address-key \"peer-a\" 9001)\n   277\t          att-b (udp/address-key \"peer-b\" 9002)\n   278\t          project-a (udp/projection {:attachment att-a :traffic traffic\n   279\t                                     :cursor cursor :ring channel-a})\n   280\t          project-b (udp/projection {:attachment att-b :traffic traffic\n   281\t                                     :cursor cursor :ring channel-b})]\n   282\t      (udp/receive! port \"peer-a\" 9001 (cbor/encode (request \"s\" 1 \"a\")))\n   283\t      (udp/receive! port \"peer-b\" 9002 (cbor/encode (request \"s\" 2 \"b\")))\n   284\t      (udp/step! project-a)\n   285\t      (udp/step! project-b)\n   286\t      (is (= [(request \"s\" 1 \"a\")] (values channel-a)))\n   287\t      (is (= [(request \"s\" 2 \"b\")] (values channel-b))))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"I found a likely boundary defect: the receiver compares declared part count times 1200 against the 64 KiB message limit. That estimate can exceed the actual encoded message size. I’m checking the send and receive paths together to pin down the affected range and any other sign-off issues."}}
